.TH GREPNET 1 "October 13, 2026" "grepnet 5.0.0" "User Commands"
.ad l
.nh


.SH NAME

grepnet - Network-aware grep for CIDR blocks, IP ranges, and network addresses

.SH SYNOPSIS

\fBgrepnet\fR [\fIOPTION\fR...] \fIPATTERN\fR [\fIFILE\fR...]

\fBgrepnet\fR [\fIOPTION\fR...] \fB-f\fR \fIPATTERN_FILE\fR [\fIFILE\fR...]

.SH DESCRIPTION

grepnet searches for network addresses in text files using network algebra instead of substring matching. A pattern like \fB192.0.2.0/24\fR will match any IP address, CIDR block, subnet mask, or IP range that falls within that network—regardless of format.

This makes grepnet ideal for:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNetwork auditing\fR: Find all references to a network across multiple files and formats
.IP \(bu 2
\fBConfiguration review\fR: Locate which systems/applications reference specific networks
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSecurity analysis\fR: Identify networks referenced in logs, configs, and documentation
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNetwork extraction\fR: Extract and consolidate all networks mentioned in files

.SS Core Concept: Network Matching

.RE
Unlike grep which does text matching, grepnet understands network topology. These all represent the same /24 network:

.EX
192.0.2.0/24             (CIDR)
192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0  (Subnet mask)
192.0.2.0 0.0.0.255      (Inverse mask)
192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255    (IP range)
192.0.2.1                (Single IP within the network)
.EE
A single \fBgrepnet '192.0.2.0/24'\fR command finds all of these.

.SH QUICK START

.EX
.SH Find any reference to a network

grepnet '192.0.2.0/24' config.txt

.SH Search from stdin

echo "server 192.0.2.1" | grepnet '192.0.2.0/24'

.SH Search multiple files recursively

grepnet -r '10.0.0.0/8' /etc/

.SH Show line numbers and only matching networks

grepnet -n -o '192.0.2.0/24' data.txt

.SH Extract all networks from a file

grepnet --extract config.txt

.SH Consolidate overlapping networks

grepnet --consolidate networks.txt
.EE
.SH PATTERN AND FILE INPUT

\fIPATTERN\fR
: Network pattern to search for (CIDR, IP range, subnet mask, bare IP, or file glob with \fB-f\fR)

\fIFILE\fR...
: Files to search. If omitted, reads from stdin.

.TP
\fB-P\fR, \fB--pattern\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
Specify additional patterns (repeatable: \fB-P net1 -P net2\fR)

.TP
\fB-f\fR, \fB--patterns-file\fR \fIPATTERN_FILE\fR
Read patterns from file, one per line (repeatable)

.TP
\fB-F\fR, \fB--input-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Specify input file explicitly (alternative to positional FILE arguments)

.SH MATCH TYPES

By default, grepnet finds all matches. Use these flags to filter by relationship type:

.TP
\fB-e\fR, \fB--equal\fR
Match only networks that are exactly equal

.TP
\fB-s\fR, \fB--subset\fR
Match only when file network is smaller than pattern (contained within)

.TP
\fB-S\fR, \fB--superset\fR
Match only when file network is larger than pattern (contains pattern)

.TP
\fB-O\fR, \fB--overlap\fR
Match only when networks partially overlap

.TP
\fB-u\fR, \fB--unmatched\fR
Include networks that don't match any pattern (for use with other match type flags)

.TP
\fB-v\fR, \fB--invert-match\fR
Show lines that do NOT match (inverse logic)

\fBExample\fR: \fBgrepnet -s '192.0.0.0/16' data.txt\fR finds all /24 or /25 networks within 192.0.0.0/16

.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS

.TP
\fB-o\fR, \fB--only-matching\fR
Print only the matched networks, not the entire line

.TP
\fB-n\fR, \fB--line-number\fR
Prefix output with 1-based line numbers

.TP
\fB-b\fR, \fB--byte-offset\fR
Prefix output with 0-based byte offsets

.TP
\fB-H\fR, \fB--with-filename\fR
Include filename in output (default when multiple files)

\fB--no-filename\fR
: Suppress filename in output

.TP
\fB-T\fR, \fB--initial-tab\fR
Insert tab after line number/byte offset for alignment

.TP
\fB-c\fR, \fB--count\fR
Print count of matching lines instead of lines

.TP
\fB-l\fR, \fB--files-with-matches\fR
Print only filenames containing matches (useful for scripting)

.TP
\fB-L\fR, \fB--files-without-match\fR
Print only filenames without matches

.TP
\fB-q\fR, \fB--quiet\fR, \fB--silent\fR
Suppress output (exit code only)

\fB--label\fR \fILABEL\fR
: Display stdin as \fILABEL\fR instead of "(standard input)"

.TP
\fB-m\fR \fINUM\fR, \fB--max-count\fR \fINUM\fR
Stop searching after \fINUM\fR matches

.SH CONTEXT OPTIONS

.TP
\fB-A\fR \fINUM\fR, \fB--after-context\fR \fINUM\fR
Print \fINUM\fR lines after each match

.TP
\fB-B\fR \fINUM\fR, \fB--before-context\fR \fINUM\fR
Print \fINUM\fR lines before each match

.TP
\fB-C\fR \fINUM\fR, \fB--context\fR \fINUM\fR
Print \fINUM\fR lines before and after each match

\fB--group-separator\fR \fISEP\fR
: Print \fISEP\fR between match groups (default: "--")

\fB--no-group-separator\fR
: Suppress group separator

\fBExample\fR: \fBgrepnet -C 2 '192.0.2.0/24' config.txt\fR shows each match with 2 lines of context

.SH EXTRACTION AND CONSOLIDATION

\fB--extract\fR
: Extract all unique networks found in input (deduplicated)

\fB--consolidate\fR
: Consolidate overlapping and adjacent networks into larger blocks

\fB--fill-pct\fR \fIN\fR
: Allow consolidation to fill gaps up to N% of network size (only with \fB--consolidate\fR; range 0-100)

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Finds the smallest supernet for each network where gap_ratio ≤ N%
.IP \(bu 2
Single-pass: fill-pct applies once per extracted network, then networks merge via \fBcollapse_addresses()\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Examples: \fB--fill-pct=50\fR allows up to 50% gap filling; \fB--fill-pct=0\fR disallows any gap

.RE
\fB--show-content\fR
: Include original line content in extract/consolidate output

\fB--format\fR {\fBcidr\fR|\fBrange\fR|\fBshort-range\fR|\fBips\fR|\fBoriginal\fR}
: Output format for networks (default: \fBcidr\fR)

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcidr\fR - CIDR notation: \fB192.0.2.0/24\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrange\fR - Full ranges: \fB192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshort-range\fR - Abbreviated ranges: \fB192.0.2.0-255\fR (IPv4 only, last octet)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBips\fR - Individual IP addresses, one per line (only with \fB--consolidate\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoriginal\fR - Original format as found (not available with \fB--consolidate\fR)

.RE
\fBExamples\fR:

.EX
.SH Extract all unique networks

grepnet --extract network_logs.txt

.SH Consolidate overlapping networks into larger blocks

grepnet --consolidate all_networks.txt

.SH Consolidate with gap filling (up to 50%)

grepnet --consolidate --fill-pct=50 network_ranges.txt

.SH Extract networks matching a pattern, output as ranges

grepnet --extract '10.0.0.0/8' systems.txt --format range

.SH Consolidate and output individual IPs

grepnet --consolidate --format ips networks.txt

.SH Consolidate and show which lines contained each network

grepnet --consolidate --show-content config.txt

.SH Combine fill-pct with IP list output

grepnet --consolidate --fill-pct=50 --format ips network_ranges.txt
.EE
.SH FILTERING

\fB--exclude-string\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
: Exclude lines matching text pattern (regex; repeatable)

\fB--exclude-strings-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
: Load text patterns to exclude from file

\fB--exclude-cidr\fR \fINETWORK\fR
: Exclude specific networks (repeatable)

\fB--exclude-cidrs-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
: Load networks to exclude from file

\fB--exclude-cidr-behaviour\fR {subset|superset|equals|related|cut}
: How to match excluded networks (default: subset)

.RS
Control how excluded networks are matched:
.TP
\fBsubset\fR
Exclude networks contained within excluded CIDR (default)
.TP
\fBsuperset\fR
Exclude networks that contain excluded CIDR
.TP
\fBequals\fR
Exclude only exact matches
.TP
\fBrelated\fR
Exclude any network that touches, contains, or is contained by excluded CIDR
.TP
\fBcut\fR
Remove excluded network and return non-overlapping remainder blocks
.RE

.SS Examples:
.RS
\fB--exclude-cidr-behaviour=subset --exclude-cidr 192.0.0.0/16\fR excludes 192.0.2.0/24 but not 10.0.0.0/8

\fB--exclude-cidr-behaviour=cut --exclude-cidr 192.0.2.4/30 192.0.2.0/24\fR returns gap-fill blocks
.RE

\fBExample\fR: \fBgrepnet '192.0.0.0/16' data.txt --exclude-string 'deprecated'\fR finds all matches except lines marked deprecated

.SH FILE DISCOVERY

.TP
\fB-r\fR, \fB--recursive\fR
Search directories recursively (don't follow symlinks)

.TP
\fB-R\fR, \fB--dereference-recursive\fR
Search directories recursively (follow symlinks)

\fB--include\fR \fIGLOB\fR
: Include only files matching glob (repeatable)

\fB--exclude\fR \fIGLOB\fR
: Exclude files matching glob (repeatable)

\fB--exclude-from\fR \fIFILE\fR
: Load exclude patterns from file

\fB--exclude-dir\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
: Exclude directories matching glob (repeatable)

\fBExamples\fR:

.EX
.SH Search all .conf files recursively

grepnet -r --include '*.conf' '192.0.0.0/16' /etc/

.SH Search everywhere except backups

grepnet -r --exclude-dir 'backup' '10.0.0.0/8' /var/

.SH Search logs excluding old archive subdirectories

grepnet -r --exclude '*/old/*' '192.0.2.0/24' /var/log/
.EE
.SH PARSING CONTROL

\fB--parse-cidr\fR {\fBpattern\fR|\fBinput\fR|\fBboth\fR|\fBnone\fR}
: Accept CIDR notation in patterns, input, both, or neither (default: \fBboth\fR)

\fB--parse-netmask\fR {\fBpattern\fR|\fBinput\fR|\fBboth\fR|\fBnone\fR}
: Accept subnet masks (default: \fBboth\fR)

\fB--parse-hostmask\fR {\fBpattern\fR|\fBinput\fR|\fBboth\fR|\fBnone\fR}
: Accept inverse/host masks (default: \fBboth\fR)

\fB--parse-ranges\fR {\fBpattern\fR|\fBinput\fR|\fBboth\fR|\fBnone\fR}
: Accept IP ranges (default: \fBboth\fR)

\fB--range-format\fR {\fBlong\fR|\fBshort\fR|\fBboth\fR|\fBnone\fR}
: Accept long/short range formats (default: \fBboth\fR)

Use these to restrict which network formats are recognized. For example, \fB--parse-ranges none\fR ignores range notation entirely.

.SH PREFIX LENGTH FILTERING

\fB--ipv4-shorter-than\fR \fIN\fR
: Include only IPv4 networks with prefix length < N

\fB--ipv4-longer-than\fR \fIN\fR
: Include only IPv4 networks with prefix length > N

\fB--ipv6-shorter-than\fR \fIN\fR
: Include only IPv6 networks with prefix length < N

\fB--ipv6-longer-than\fR \fIN\fR
: Include only IPv6 networks with prefix length > N

Filter extracted or consolidated networks by prefix length (CIDR mask size). Useful for finding smaller subnets or larger aggregates.

.SS Examples:
.RS
\fB--ipv4-longer-than=24\fR - Only networks with /25 or smaller (more specific)

\fB--ipv4-shorter-than=16\fR - Only networks with /0 to /15 (less specific)

\fB--ipv4-longer-than=24 --ipv4-shorter-than=32\fR - Networks between /25 and /31

\fB--ipv6-longer-than=64\fR - Only IPv6 with /65 or longer
.RE

.SH STRICT CIDR VALIDATION

\fB--strict-cidr\fR {\fBpattern\fR|\fBinput\fR|\fBboth\fR|\fBnone\fR}
: Apply strict CIDR validation (default: \fBnone\fR)

\fB--strict-cidr-action\fR {\fBfail\fR|\fBnormalize\fR|\fBignore\fR|\fBwarn\fR}
: Action on invalid CIDR (default: \fBnormalize\fR)

Strict mode rejects CIDRs with host bits set (e.g., \fB192.0.2.1/24\fR should be \fB192.0.2.0/24\fR).

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfail\fR - Exit with error code 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnormalize\fR - Silently correct to network address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBignore\fR - Skip invalid CIDR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBwarn\fR - Print warning, then skip

.SH BINARY FILE HANDLING

.TP
\fB-a\fR, \fB--text\fR
Treat all files as text

.RE
\fB-I\fR
: Skip binary files

\fB--binary-files\fR {\fBbinary\fR|\fBtext\fR|\fBwithout-match\fR}
: How to handle binary files (default: \fBbinary\fR)

.SH COMMENT FILTERING

By default, grepnet ignores commented lines and removes inline comments from processing.

\fB--no-filter-comments\fR
: Include commented lines in search

Recognized comment markers: \fB#\fR, \fB!\fR, \fB--\fR, \fB//\fR, \fB;\fR

Cisco keywords like \fBremark\fR and \fBdescription\fR are also recognized as comment markers.

\fBExample\fR:

.EX
.SH Default behavior: this line is ignored

server 192.0.2.1 # production

.SH With --no-filter-comments: both lines included

.EE
.SH COLOR OUTPUT

\fB--color\fR {\fBauto\fR|\fBalways\fR|\fBnever\fR}
: Use colored output (default: \fBauto\fR - colors on terminal, none when piped)

\fBGREPNET_COLORS\fR
: Environment variable to customize colors (space-separated \fBKEY=ANSI_CODE\fR pairs)

.EX
export GREPNET_COLORS="match=01;32 filename=35"
grepnet '192.0.2.0/24' *.txt
.EE
.SH DATA FORMATS

.TP
\fB-z\fR, \fB--null-data\fR
Treat input/output as NUL-terminated records instead of newlines

.TP
\fB-Z\fR, \fB--null\fR
Output NUL after filename instead of colon

Useful for safe handling of filenames with special characters: \fBgrepnet -lZ '192.0.0.0/16' *.txt | xargs -0 cat\fR

.SH DEBUGGING

\fB--debug\fR [\fIFUNCTIONS\fR]
: Enable debug output to stderr

\fB--no-messages\fR
: Suppress error and warning messages

\fB--version\fR
: Show version and exit

.TP
\fB-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
Show help and exit

.SH EXIT STATUS

0
: Matches found

1
: No matches found, or no matching files found (with \fB-l\fR / \fB-L\fR)

2
: Error occurred (invalid arguments, file not found, pattern error, etc.)

.SH EXAMPLES

.SS Basic Searching

.EX
.SH Find all references to a network in a file

grepnet '192.0.2.0/24' config.txt

.SH Match a single IP against a CIDR pattern

echo "192.0.2.50" | grepnet '192.0.2.0/24'

.SH Search multiple files

grepnet '10.0.0.0/8' server1.txt server2.txt server3.txt
.EE
.SS Multiple Patterns

.EX
.SH Search for either network

grepnet -P '192.0.2.0/24' -P '10.0.0.0/8' data.txt

.SH Read patterns from file

grepnet -f allowed_networks.txt config.txt
.EE
.SS Match Type Filtering

.EX
.SH Find only exact matches

grepnet -e '192.0.2.0/24' networks.txt

.SH Find networks contained within the pattern

grepnet -s '192.0.0.0/16' all_networks.txt

.SH Find networks that contain the pattern

grepnet -S '192.0.2.1' subnets.txt
.EE
.SS Output Formatting

.EX
.SH Show line numbers

grepnet -n '192.0.2.0/24' config.txt

.SH Show only the matched networks

grepnet -o '192.0.2.0/24' data.txt

.SH Count matching lines

grepnet -c '192.0.2.0/24' *.txt

.SH Show only filenames with matches

grepnet -l '192.0.0.0/16' *.conf
.EE
.SS Context Display

.EX
.SH Show 2 lines of context around matches

grepnet -C 2 '192.0.2.0/24' config.txt

.SH Show 1 line before, 3 lines after

grepnet -B 1 -A 3 '192.0.2.0/24' audit.log

.SH Context without group separator

grepnet -C 1 --no-group-separator '192.0.2.0/24' file.txt
.EE
.SS Network Extraction

.EX
.SH Extract all unique networks from file

grepnet --extract firewall_rules.txt

.SH Extract networks matching a pattern

grepnet --extract -P '192.0.0.0/16' all_networks.txt

.SH Consolidate overlapping networks

grepnet --consolidate all_subnets.txt

.SH Output consolidated networks as ranges

grepnet --consolidate --format range networks.txt

.SH Extract and show source lines

grepnet --extract --show-content config.txt
.EE
.SS Exclusion

.EX
.SH Exclude deprecated networks

grepnet --exclude-string 'deprecated' '192.0.0.0/16' config.txt

.SH Exclude specific network from results

grepnet --exclude-cidr '192.0.2.0/25' '192.0.2.0/24' data.txt

.SH Exclude networks from file

grepnet --exclude-cidrs-file banned.txt '0.0.0.0/0' networks.txt
.EE
.SS Recursive Searching

.EX
.SH Find all references in configuration directory

grepnet -r '192.0.0.0/16' /etc/

.SH Include only .conf and .yaml files

grepnet -r --include '*.conf' --include '*.yaml' '10.0.0.0/8' /etc/

.SH Exclude backup directories

grepnet -r --exclude-dir 'backup' '192.0.2.0/24' /var/

.SH Search logs, exclude archived subdirectories

grepnet -r --exclude '*/archive/*' '192.0.0.0/16' /var/log/
.EE
.SS Binary File Handling

.EX
.SH Process binary files as text

grepnet -a '192.0.2.0/24' data.bin

.SH Report binary matches without displaying content

grepnet --binary-files without-match '192.0.0.0/16' *.bin
.EE
.SS Advanced Combinations

.EX
.SH Extract networks from pattern file, exclude deprecated, consolidate

grepnet --extract -f allowed.txt --exclude-strings-file deprecated.txt \
  --consolidate --format range config.txt

.SH Recursive search with context, line numbers, and colors

grepnet -rn -C 2 --color always '192.0.2.0/24' /etc/

.SH Find networks in logs, exclude internal, show counts per file

grepnet -rc --exclude-cidr '10.0.0.0/8' '192.0.0.0/16' /var/log/*.txt
.EE
.SH COMMON PATTERNS

\fBSecurity audits\fR:
.EX
.SH Find all external networks referenced

grepnet -r --exclude-cidr '10.0.0.0/8' --exclude-cidr '172.16.0.0/12' \
  --exclude-cidr '192.168.0.0/16' '0.0.0.0/0' /etc/
.EE
\fBConfiguration extraction\fR:
.EX
.SH Extract all networks from config files and consolidate

find /etc -name '*.conf' | xargs grepnet --extract --consolidate --format range
.EE
\fBNetwork monitoring\fR:
.EX
.SH Check for changes to allowed networks

grepnet --extract -f current.txt config.txt > new.txt
diff allowed.txt new.txt
.EE
\fBCompliance checking\fR:
.EX
.SH Verify no systems reference banned networks

if grepnet --exclude-cidrs-file banned.txt '0.0.0.0/0' *.txt > /dev/null; then
  echo "FAIL: Banned networks found"
fi
.EE
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

\fBGREPNET_COLORS\fR
.RS
Customize output colors (space-separated KEY=ANSI_CODE pairs).
.RE

.SS Supported color keys:
.TP
\fBfn\fR
Filename (default: 35 = magenta)
.TP
\fBln\fR
Line number (default: 32 = green)
.TP
\fBbn\fR
Byte offset (default: 32 = green)
.TP
\fBse\fR
Separator (default: 36 = cyan)
.TP
\fBsl\fR
Selected line background (default: 0 = none)
.TP
\fBcx\fR
Context line (default: 0 = none)
.TP
\fBmt\fR
Match text color (default: 0 = none)
.TP
\fBms\fR
Match separator (default: 0 = none)
.TP
\fBmc\fR
Match colon (default: 0 = none)
.TP
\fBme\fR
EQUALS match type (default: 01;32 = bright green)
.TP
\fBmb\fR
SUBSET match type (default: 01;34 = bright blue)
.TP
\fBmS\fR
SUPERSET match type (default: 01;33 = bright yellow)
.TP
\fBmo\fR
OVERLAP match type (default: 01;31 = bright red)
.TP
\fBmu\fR
UNMATCHED type (default: 0 = none)
.TP
\fBmn\fR
Normalized CIDR (default: 04 = underline)

.SS ANSI codes are SGR (Select Graphic Rendition) values:
.RS
.TP
0
reset/no color
.TP
01
bold
.TP
04
underline
.TP
31-37
foreground colors (red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, white)
.TP
Combine with semicolons
01;32 = bold green
.RE

.SS Example:
.EX
export GREPNET_COLORS="fn=35 me=01;31 mb=01;36"
grepnet '192.0.2.0/24' *.txt
.EE

